## Before you start

Samsung killed OEM unlocking. If a guide tells you to flip that switch in
developer options, it's out of date. No root, no custom recovery, no
NetHunter Lite.

You can still get the ADB shell, which is uid 2000 — above normal apps, below
root. Shizuku runs it on-device instead of over a cable.

That's enough to uninstall system apps, revoke permissions the settings UI
hides, and change hidden settings. It's not enough for monitor mode, packet
injection, or anything needing a custom kernel.

Nothing here touches the system partition or trips Knox. Factory reset undoes
all of it.

## 1. Repos

Install F-Droid, then add IzzyOnDroid:

```
https://apt.izzysoft.de/fdroid/repo
```

Or open `apt.izzysoft.de/fdroid/` in a browser and use the add-repo link,
which passes the signing fingerprint along with the URL.

Obtainium is also worth having. Installs from GitHub releases directly.

**If you search and don't find these apps:** F-Droid hides anti-feature apps
by default and all three tools here have flags. Shizuku is `UpstreamNonFree`,
Canta is `NonFreeDep` and `NonFreeNet`. Settings → Anti-Features → enable
them.

## 2. Shizuku

F-Droid → Shizuku. Check the app page under Links → Source Code says
`github.com/RikkaApps/Shizuku` before installing.

Don't open it yet.

## 3. Wireless debugging

Settings → About phone → Software information → tap Build number 7 times.

Then Developer options → Wireless debugging → on.

Leave USB debugging off. Nothing here needs it.

## 4. Pairing

The pairing code times out, so read this before you start.

1. Shizuku → Pairing. A notification appears asking for a code.
2. Developer options → Wireless debugging → Pair device with pairing code.
3. Type the six digits into the Shizuku notification.
4. Back in Shizuku, hit Start.

Should say "Shizuku is running."

No notification appears → Settings → Apps → Shizuku → Notifications, allow
them.

Pairs but won't start → toggle wireless debugging off and on. You have to be
on WiFi.

Dies on its own → Settings → Apps → Shizuku → Battery → Unrestricted. One UI
kills background services aggressively.

**Reboots.** Docs say Shizuku stops on reboot unless you're rooted. Mine
didn't — it came back on its own, apparently the Android 13+ trusted-WiFi
auto-start. Test yours instead of assuming.

If yours doesn't survive and you care, there's a one-time USB grant:

```bash
adb shell pm grant moe.shizuku.privileged.api android.permission.WRITE_SECURE_SETTINGS
```

People report mixed results on whether it sticks. I didn't need it.

## 5. Shell app

Install **aShell You** (`in.hridayan.ashell`, DP-Hridayan). IzzyOnDroid or
Obtainium.

Two things I got wrong first:

**LADB doesn't work with Shizuku.** Its README says so outright — they fight
over the same wireless debugging socket. Repo's archived anyway. Most guides
still recommend it.

**aShell and aShell You are different apps.** aShell is sunilpaulmathew,
aShell You is DP-Hridayan and is a fork with more backends and more recent
updates. Neither is malicious, but check which one you're installing.

On first launch pick **Local ADB**, not "ADB via Wireless Debugging." The
second one makes aShell You pair its own connection and you end up in the
same socket fight as LADB.

## 6. Verify

```
whoami
```

`shell` means it worked. Anything like `u0_a783` is the app's own UID and
means Shizuku didn't authorize it.

I got the UID first. Force stopping aShell You and reopening it fixed it —
apparently it can launch before Shizuku's service is ready and give up
silently. If that doesn't work, clear its data and let it run setup again.

Shizuku's "Authorized applications" counter said 0 the entire time, including
after everything was working. Ignore it.

## 7. The user 150 error

Run this before removing anything:

```
pm list packages | grep facebook
```

You'll probably get your results plus:

```
Error: java.lang.SecurityException: Shell does not have permission
to access user 150
```

Nothing's broken. `pm list packages` walks every user profile on the device.
User 0 is you, user 150 is a Knox container — Secure Folder or a work
profile. Shell can't see into it, which is the point of Knox.

Scope your commands and it goes away:

```
pm list packages --user 0 | grep facebook
```

Use `--user 0` on everything from here.

## 8. Canta

Install Canta (`io.github.samolego.canta`, samolego). In both F-Droid and
IzzyOnDroid.

It pulls badges from the Universal Debloat List, which is a maintained
database of what's safe to pull and what breaks. The badges are the reason to
use Canta over doing this by hand.

Removal is `pm uninstall --user 0` — the package goes away for your user, the
APK stays in the read-only system partition. Factory reset restores it.

**Leave "reset to factory version" unchecked.** That rolls an updated system
app back to its shipped version instead of removing it. Different thing.

## 9. What to remove

Three conditions, all of them: Canta badges it Recommended, you know what it
is, and you don't use it.

The badge handles dependencies you can't check yourself. You handle whether
you actually use the thing. Removing something you use is a ten-second
reinstall. Removing something with a dependency you didn't know about is the
one that hurts.

### Don't touch

Anything with Knox in the name. `com.sec.spp.push` — Samsung Push Service,
kills notifications for every Samsung app. One UI Home, which is your
launcher fallback. Samsung Account.
`com.samsung.android.bixby.service` — other stuff depends on it, though
`bixby.agent` and `bixby.wakeup` are both fine to remove. SmartThings if you
have Samsung devices paired. Anything `com.android.*` or `com.sec.android.*`
you can't identify.

### Safe on my device

Check badges on yours, carrier and region change what ships.

```
com.facebook.appmanager
com.facebook.services
com.facebook.system
com.facebook.orca
com.facebook.katana

com.google.android.adservices.api    # Privacy Sandbox / Topics API
com.google.android.apps.tachyon      # Meet

com.samsung.android.app.spage        # Samsung Free
com.samsung.android.app.dailyboard
com.samsung.sree                     # Global Goals
com.samsung.android.app.tips
com.samsung.android.service.peoplestripe
com.samsung.android.bixby.agent
com.samsung.android.bixby.wakeup
com.samsung.android.aremoji
com.samsung.android.aremojieditor
com.samsung.android.arzone
```

The Facebook packages are preinstalled whether or not you ever install
Facebook.

LinkedIn, Netflix, Spotify, Office, Amazon are all removable if you don't use
them. They ship as stubs that pull the real app from Play when tapped, so if
you use them you already installed over the stub.

Two I kept. `com.google.android.gms.supervision` is a GMS internal and those
tend to come back on Play system updates, plus if it breaks something you
won't notice for weeks. And AR Emoji / AR Zone, because I use the camera
modes — if you remove `aremoji` but keep `arzone` you get a button that
errors, so do both or neither.

### Batches

Five to ten at a time, then reboot and check the phone still works. Short
suspect list if something breaks.

Canta's README puts the actual risk plainly: remove something critical and
you can bootloop into a factory reset. `install-existing` doesn't help on a
phone that won't boot.

### The list lies sometimes

After I pulled the Facebook packages Canta still showed `com.facebook.orca`.
Checked in the shell, nothing there. It had worked, the list was just cached.
Verify in the shell when something looks off.

## 10. Cleanup

Reboot. Check the phone boots, notifications come through, camera opens, and
run `whoami` to see if Shizuku survived.

When you're done for the session: Shizuku → menu → Stop, and turn wireless
debugging back off. It's not that an idle Shizuku is dangerous — only
authorized apps can reach it — but there's no reason to leave a privileged
service running when nothing's using it.

Anything you grant Shizuku access to can run commands as `shell`. Grant it
like you'd grant a root terminal.

### Where to stop

Most of the benefit is the first ten or fifteen packages. Past that you're
chasing a few hundred MB against a real chance of a subtle bug you won't
trace back here.

Some things fail loudly — pull Samsung Push Service and you'll know within an
hour. Ad frameworks and GMS internals fail quietly, and you find out three
weeks later when something unrelated acts up.

## FAQ

**Voids warranty / trips Knox?** No.

**Will removed apps come back?** GMS components do, on Play system updates.
Samsung apps can come back on firmware updates. Re-remove them.

**Pixel / OnePlus / Xiaomi?** Shizuku part works, package lists don't. Use
Canta's badges.

**Need a computer?** No. The USB grant in step 4 is optional and most people
won't need it.

**Bootloop?** Factory reset. Everything comes back.

**Is this root?** No. Shell is uid 2000, root is uid 0.

## Commands

```bash
whoami                                    # want: shell
pm list packages --user 0
pm list packages --user 0 | grep <term>
pm list packages -d --user 0              # removed/disabled
pm uninstall --user 0 <package>
cmd package install-existing <package>    # undo
logcat
```

